\begin{note}
\emph{Mutual recursion} is when two (or more) functions call each other,
forming a cycle of recursive calls.
The base case of a mutually recursive pair may live in only one of the functions,
so long as every call into the other function is eventually driven back
to that base case.
Mutual recursion is a natural fit for problems that alternate between two modes,
such as summing the elements at even and odd indices of a list,
or deciding whether a number is even or odd.
\end{note}
